{
  "id": "Compound::DB00262 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Carmustine, a well-established antineoplastic agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of hematologic cancer through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Carmustine may contribute to the development of hematologic cancer via the formation of cerebral hematoma or idiopathic pneumonia syndrome, both of which are linked to other agents such as Dasatinib and Thiotepa, respectively. These agents are themselves antineoplastic agents that treat hematologic cancer, indicating a potential shared therapeutic target or mechanism. The presence of organochlorine and nitrosourea compounds as common ancestors further supports the classification of Carmustine as an antineoplastic agent, reinforcing its role in the pathophysiology of hematologic cancer. The integrated evidence suggests that Carmustine may exert its effects through mechanisms that contribute to the progression of hematologic cancer, though the exact nature of these mechanisms remains to be fully elucidated.",
